Multiplying the two ladder operators also displays the quantum correction to the classical energy: $\begin{aligned}
N
&=\frac{1}{2\omega}(p+i\omega x)(p-i\omega x)\\
&=\frac{1}{2\omega}\left(p^{2}+\omega^{2}x^{2}
 +i\omega[x,p]\right)\\
&=\frac{1}{2\omega}\left(p^{2}+\omega^{2}x^{2}-\omega\right),\end{aligned}$ where $[x,p]=i$. Consequently, $H=\omega\left(N+\frac12\right).$ If the additive constant is irrelevant to the problem, it is convenient to use the shifted Hamiltonian $H_{\mathrm{shifted}}=H-\frac{\omega}{2}=\omega N.$ The term $\omega/2$ is the oscillator’s zero-point, or ground-state, energy. For a field mode the analogous term is called vacuum energy. It reflects the uncertainty principle: no state can have both $x$ and $p$ sharply equal to zero, so the two positive quadratic terms in $H$ cannot both have zero expectation value.

