p.src:
This conclusion identifies two exchange eigenspaces, but it does not say that every arbitrary two-particle wavefunction is already an eigenvector of $S$. Any such wavefunction can be separated into its two parts, $\psi_{\pm}=\frac12(I\pm S)\psi,$ which obey $S\psi_{\pm}=\pm\psi_{\pm}.$ Thus $\begin{aligned}
\psi_{+}(x_{2},x_{1})&=\psi_{+}(x_{1},x_{2}),\\
\psi_{-}(x_{2},x_{1})&=-\psi_{-}(x_{1},x_{2}).\end{aligned}$

